            ("What type of rock forms from the cooling and solidification of molten magma beneath the surface or lava on Earth's surface?", "Igneous rock", ["Sedimentary rock", "Metamorphic rock", "Conglomerate rock"], "Examples include intrusive granite (cooled slowly underground) and extrusive basalt (cooled rapidly on the surface)."),
            ("What type of rock forms from the accumulation, compaction, and cementation of mineral particles, organic debris, and sediments?", "Sedimentary rock", ["Igneous rock", "Metamorphic rock", "Volcanic tuff"], "Sandstone, limestone, and shale are classic sedimentary rocks, frequently containing fossilized organism imprints."),
            ("What type of rock forms when pre-existing rocks undergo profound physical and chemical alteration from intense heat and pressure?", "Metamorphic rock", ["Igneous rock", "Sedimentary rock", "Extrusive basalt"], "Metamorphic processes transform limestone into marble, shale into slate, and granite into banded gneiss."),
            ("What is the scientific term for the circular ocean surface current systems driven by global wind patterns and Earth's Coriolis effect?", "Oceanic Gyres", ["Thermohaline currents", "Rip currents", "Tidal bores"], "The five major subtropical gyres (North/South Pacific, North/South Atlantic, Indian Ocean) concentrate floating marine debris."),
            ("What deep ocean circulatory conveyor belt is driven by global density gradients created by surface heat and freshwater salinity differences?", "Thermohaline Circulation (The Global Ocean Conveyor)", ["The Coriolis System", "The Gulf Stream gyre", "The Ekman Spiral"], "Cold, saline water sinks in the North Atlantic and flows through deep ocean basins worldwide over a 1,000-year cycle."),
            ("What warm, swift Atlantic ocean current originates in the Gulf of Mexico, moderating the climate of Western Europe?", "The Gulf Stream", ["The California Current", "The Canary Current", "The Humboldt Current"], "The Gulf Stream transports over 30 million cubic meters of warm tropical water per second northeastward across the Atlantic.")
